#region Part02
// 1. Difference between Class and Struct:
// - Class: Reference type stored on Heap, supports inheritance, can be null.
// - Struct: Value type stored on Stack, performance-optimized, no class inheritance.

// 2. Class Relationships (Other than Inheritance):
// - Association: Simple "uses-a" link between independent objects.
// - Aggregation: "has-a" relationship where child objects can exist without the parent.
// - Composition: "has-a" relationship where child lifecycle is strictly tied to the parent.
// - Dependency: Temporary usage of a class inside a method scope.
#endregion

#region Part03-Bonus 

// 01.01. Why Structs Do Not Support Inheritance??
// - Stack vs Heap Memory: Structs are value types stored on the Stack (or inline) 
//   with a fixed memory size known at compile time.
// - No Object Overhead: Classes use dynamic dispatch and store a Type Handle and 
//   Virtual Method Table (VTable) in their memory header on the Heap. Structs lack 
//   this overhead to stay lightweight.
// - Memory Slicing Prevention: Inheriting structs would cause field-slicing and memory 
//   corruption when assigning a child struct to a parent struct variable on the Stack.
// * Note: Structs implicitly inherit from System.ValueType and can implement interfaces.

// 01.02. Execution Behavior: Product() vs Sum() (Parent vs Child)??
// - Product() calls Parent (Early / Static Binding):
//   The method uses the 'new' keyword for method hiding. The compiler resolves the call 
//   at compile time strictly based on the reference type (Parent P01).
// - Sum() calls Child (Late / Dynamic Binding):
//   The method uses 'virtual' and 'override'. The resolution is deferred to runtime, 
//   where the Virtual Method Table (VTable) invokes the implementation of the actual object 
//   instance on the Heap (Child Ch01).

// 01.03. Static vs Dynamic Binding??
// - Static (Early) Binding: Resolved at compile time based on the reference type. 
//   Offers faster performance. Used for regular methods, overloaded methods, and 'new' methods.
// - Dynamic (Late) Binding: Resolved at runtime based on the actual instance type. 
//   Uses a VTable lookup to enable true runtime polymorphism via 'virtual' and 'override'.

// 0.01. Static vs Dynamic Binding:
// - Static Binding (Early): Resolved at compile-time. Fast execution. Used for static methods, 
//   overloaded methods, and non-virtual method hiding ('new').
// - Dynamic Binding (Late): Resolved at runtime using Virtual Method Tables (vtable). 
//   Enables true polymorphism via 'virtual' and 'override' keywords.
#endregion

